(Title) Tj
T*
(Feasibility and Community Uptake of HIV Prevention Services Delivered Through Barbershops) Tj
T*
(in a High-Transmission Region of Uganda: Twelve-Month Results) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Identifying acceptable venues for HIV prevention engagement remains critical in high-) Tj
T*
(burden settings. We randomized 12 barbershops in an urban district to deliver an) Tj
T*
(integrated HIV prevention program and 6 control barbershops providing standard services.) Tj
T*
(Approximately 250 men \(mean age 34 years\) were enrolled. The intervention achieved high) Tj
T*
(feasibility: 94% of enrolled participants attended at least one educational session, 87%) Tj
T*
(accepted self-testing kits, and 76% completed the offered peer-discussion group.) Tj
T*
(Acceptability was strong, with 89% of participants rating the program as acceptable or) Tj
T*
(highly acceptable. Among the intervention group, 68% initiated HIV testing during the) Tj
T*
(study period compared with 42% in control barbershops \(relative risk 1.62; 95% CI) Tj
T*
(1.15-2.28\). Reported sexual risk behaviors showed greater reductions in the intervention) Tj
T*
(arm \(29% reduction vs. 8% in controls; p=0.03\). Interest in long-acting preexposure) Tj
T*
(prophylaxis increased in the intervention group \(23% expressing interest vs. 12% in) Tj
T*
(controls; p<0.001\). Barbershops proved a feasible and acceptable venue for reaching men) Tj
T*
(with HIV prevention services.) Tj
